    "q-216636": {
      "dom_id": "step-q216636",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The solution to $\\frac{9}{2x}=3$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution. This is because if $x=0,$ then the rational term would be $\\frac{9}{2\\cdot 0},$ which is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by $(2x)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2x} & =3 \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\frac{9}{2x} & =3\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{9}{\\cancel{2x}} & =6x \\\\ 9 & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =6x \\\\ \\frac{9}{6} & =\\frac{6x}{6} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
