    "q-216650": {
      "dom_id": "step-q216650",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "The solution to $2=\\frac{6x}{x+10}$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=-10$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(x+10)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{6x}{x+10} \\\\ (x+10)\\cdot 2 & =(x+10)\\cdot \\frac{6x}{x+10} \\\\ (x+10)\\cdot 2 & =\\cancel{(x+10)}\\cdot \\frac{6x}{\\cancel{x+10}} \\\\ 2x+20 & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+20 & =6x \\\\ 2x+20-2x & =6x-2x \\\\ 20 & =4x \\\\ \\frac{20}{4} & =\\frac{4x}{4} \\\\ 5 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x=5$ is different from $x=-10$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
