    "q-216640": {
      "dom_id": "step-q216640",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The solution to $\\frac{6x}{x+4}=3$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=-4$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(x+4)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6x}{x+4} & =3 \\\\ (x+4)\\cdot \\frac{6x}{x+4} & =(x+4)\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\cancel{(x+4)}\\cdot \\frac{6x}{\\cancel{x+4}} & =(x+4)\\cdot 3 \\\\ 6x & =3x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & =3x+12 \\\\ 6x-3x & =3x+12-3x \\\\ 3x & =12 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=4$ is different from $x=-4$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
