    "q-216726": {
      "dom_id": "step-q216726",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "The solution to $\\frac{7x+4}{2x-1}=5$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(2x-1)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7x+4}{2x-1} & =5 \\\\ (2x-1)\\cdot \\frac{7x+4}{2x-1} & =(2x-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{(2x-1)}\\cdot \\frac{7x+4}{\\cancel{2x-1}} & =(2x-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\ 7x+4 & =10x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+4 & =10x-5 \\\\ 7x+4+5 & =10x-5+5 \\\\ 7x+9 & =10x \\\\ 7x+9-7x & =10x-7x \\\\ 9 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{9}{3} & =\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ 3 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=3$ is different from $x=\\frac{1}{2}$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
